<article class="env-lemma" id="00GA">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00GA">Lemma <span data-tag="00GA">10.35.9</span>.</a> Let $R \to S$ be a ring map. Let $\mathfrak m \subset R$ be a maximal ideal. Let $\mathfrak q \subset S$ be a prime ideal lying over $\mathfrak m$ such that $\kappa (\mathfrak q)/\kappa (\mathfrak m)$ is an algebraic field extension. Then $\mathfrak q$ is a maximal ideal of $S$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Consider the diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ S \ar[r] &  S/\mathfrak q \ar[r] &  \kappa (\mathfrak q) \\  R \ar[r] \ar[u] &  R/\mathfrak m \ar[u] }  \]
</div>
    <p>
       We see that $\kappa (\mathfrak m) \subset S/\mathfrak q \subset \kappa (\mathfrak q)$. Because the field extension $\kappa (\mathfrak m) \subset \kappa (\mathfrak q)$ is algebraic, any ring between $\kappa (\mathfrak m)$ and $\kappa (\mathfrak q)$ is a field (Fields, Lemma <a href="/tag/0BID" data-tag="0BID">9.8.10</a>). Thus $S/\mathfrak q$ is a field, and a posteriori equal to $\kappa (\mathfrak q)$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>